AR composition, the twin of assets/franka/ar_scene.xml: the UNMODIFIED
Menagerie so101.xml on a simple table whose top sits at z = 0 (the robot base
plane), extending down 0.73 m to the operator's physical floor. No skybox, no
ground plane: the background is passthrough. The table is smaller than the
Franka's because the arm is -- the SO-101's reach is ~0.35 m, not ~0.85 m.

THIS FILE IS THE ONLY TRACKED FILE IN THIS DIRECTORY; so101.xml and assets/ are
fetched by scripts/fetch-menagerie.sh, and the include has no path in it
because the wrapper must be a sibling of the file it includes. Both facts are
explained at the top of that script and in the Franka twin.

THE ROBOT SITS AT THE ORIGIN, exactly as the Franka does, and this is a
decision rather than an omission. A verified alternative exists and was
deliberately not taken. Putting the arm in a <frame pos="..."> requires
<attach> instead of <include>, and <attach> under MuJoCo's default conflict
policy silently DISCARDS ALL SIX authored <option> fields of so101.xml --
timestep 0.005->0.002, integrator implicitfast->Euler, cone elliptic->pyramidal,
impratio 10->1, iterations 10->100, ls_iterations 20->50. THE PAIR THAT MATTERS
IS cone/impratio, the gripper's authored friction model: so101.xml's
collision_gripper class is condim=6 with torsional friction, and elliptic cone +
impratio=10 is what makes that pinch hold. Lose those and the arm looks
identical and grasps differently. <compiler conflict="merge"/> restores all six
with a bitwise-identical trace, and is not used here because moving the robot
is a WHOLE-APP comfort question, not an SO-101 one: at the shipped calibration
the Franka's home TCP is 1.592 m from the eye and the SO-101's is 1.493 m, so
the placement argument indicts the Franka harder, and fixing it for one scene
alone is incoherent. TRIGGER, so this is a decision with an expiry: the first
headset session that reports the robot reads too small or too far away -> add
<compiler conflict="merge"/> and wrap an <attach> in a <frame pos="...">.
-->
<mujoco model="so101 AR scene">
  <include file="so101.xml"/>

  <!-- AFTER the robot, and that is the entire transparency design: mjv_updateScene
       emits in GEOM-ID ORDER (measured; tests/test_ghost.py asserts it), so a body
       declared here lands last in mjvScene and blends correctly against everything
       drawn before it. Moving this line above the include silently breaks the
       ghost -- it would still render, just wrongly composited. -->
  <include file="../leader/leader_gripper.xml"/>

  <worldbody>
    <geom name="table" type="box" size="0.32 0.30 0.365" pos="0.10 0 -0.365"
        rgba="0.46 0.36 0.25 1"/>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- so101.xml ships no keyframe, and the control step requires one named
       "home" (it is both the A-reset pose and, where ns_gain != 0, the
       nullspace bias -- though this robot's row sets ns_gain = 0).

       The four arm-plane joints are /code/Lab's Isaac-Stack-Cube-SO101 reach
       posture verbatim: they ARE that posture, and their sum is its 45.8 deg
       approach pitch. wrist_roll = pi/2 is NOT from Lab.

       IN JOINT SPACE that deviation is free: wrist_roll is distal to all four
       arm-plane joints, so it changes the jaw orientation without disturbing
       the reach posture at all. IT IS NOT FREE IN THE TASK SPACE. It is a spin
       about the WRIST axis, and the TCP does not lie on that axis:
       tcp_offset[0] = 0.012 puts it 12.0 mm off, so the TCP sweeps a 12 mm
       circle. Measured against Lab's wrist_roll = 0, TCP displacement is
       9.186 mm at pi/4, 16.973 mm at pi/2 (shipped) and 24.004 mm at pi --
       exactly sqrt(2)*12 and 2*12. THE SHIPPED HOME TCP THEREFORE SITS 17.0 mm
       FROM LAB'S. If you are comparing poses against Lab, that offset is
       expected and is here.

       Why pi/2 anyway: it rolls the jaws toward level, which is the
       orientation a table-top pick needs. Measured as the jaw-tip SEPARATION
       axis (centroid of the three fixed_jaw_sph_tip* geoms to the centroid of
       the three moving_jaw_sph_tip*, at the home aperture of 129.9 mm), it
       goes from 80.0 deg off horizontal at wrist_roll = 0 to 26.9 deg at pi/2.
       NONE OF THIS HAS BEEN CHECKED ON A DEVICE.

       qpos[gripper] MUST equal robot_spec.py's gripper_open (1.745), and ctrl=
       must be present and equal to qpos: the jaw is driven to gripper_open at
       trigger = 0, so any other value here makes every A-press twitch the jaw
       as it snaps to the commanded one. -->
  <keyframe>
    <key name="home" qpos="0 -0.6 0.8 0.6 1.5708 1.745"
         ctrl="0 -0.6 0.8 0.6 1.5708 1.745"/>
  </keyframe>
</mujoco>
